{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== SESSION HANDOFF PROMPT (paste this to start the next session) ===\n\nDrive rebar epic 061c-ecd1-8967-4a76 (LLM provider seam) to completion using the /rebar-implement\nprotocol. Worktree /Users/joeoakhart/rebar-wt-provider-seam on branch feat/llm-provider-seam.\n\n*** EXPORT REBAR_GATE_REF=HEAD IN EVERY SHELL THAT RUNS A GATE. ***\n`--ref HEAD` alone is NOT enough and WILL mislead you. The CLI flag does not govern the claim-gate\nfreshness re-check: compute_validity() re-hashes dependency files at gate_source.default_ref(),\nwhich defaults to origin/main. This branch has unmerged commits touching runner.py, so any scoped\nattestation naming it reads a FALSE \"stale-code\" without the env var and \"certified\" with it.\nProven both ways on the same ticket in one command.\n\nRead the last comments of rebar session log 55a9-ad33-3829-4e21 for the full trail.\nTRUST THE STORE OVER THIS PROMPT — query tickets and Gerrit before acting. This text ages.\n\n================================================================================\nWHAT THIS EPIC IS FOR\n================================================================================\nrebar's LLM stack had provider identity smeared across the codebase: capability decisions were made\nby sniffing provider-name strings, HTTP clients were built ad hoc and leaked, and there was no way\nto say \"use the cheap model here, the smart one there\" without hardcoding model ids in many places.\nThe epic does three things:\n\n 1. ONE PROVIDER SEAM. All provider/client construction goes through a per-run ProviderSession\n    behind pydantic-ai's infer_model, with first-class AWS Bedrock alongside direct Anthropic and a\n    best-effort OpenAI-compatible endpoint. Capability decisions read the ModelProfile, never a\n    provider-name string.\n 2. MODEL CLASSES. Three reserved names — trivial / standard / frontier — become the universal\n    vocabulary for \"which model\", replacing scattered literal ids. An operator retargets the whole\n    system (e.g. onto Bedrock) by configuring three class slots.\n 3. PROVENANCE + CUTOVER. Signed verdicts stamp which provider/endpoint actually served them, and\n    the production review bot cuts over to Bedrock Claude.\n\nWE DOGFOOD THIS. rebar's own plan-review, code-review and completion gates run on this code, so a\nregression here breaks the tooling reviewing the change. The operator's standing rule is therefore:\nland anything that will NOT introduce regression on main, and PASSING CI IS NOT SUFFICIENT EVIDENCE\nof that. LLM functionality must be preserved on THIS project's configuration specifically (we run\nbare claude-opus-4-8 for Pass-1 and claude-sonnet-4-6 for the verifier, over direct Anthropic;\nat call time those strings are provider-qualified).\n\n================================================================================\nWHERE WE STAND\n================================================================================\nMERGED TO MAIN: 10 changes (Gerrit 1012-1090) — S1 provider seam, S2 ModelProfile capabilities,\nS3 Bedrock provider, S4 OpenAI base_url, S7 review-bot Bedrock access, the 2682/3a98 module splits,\n343b provenance, 281f knob removal, 1903 temperature table.\n\nOPEN ON GERRIT, ALL SUBMITTABLE (11): 1100 (1484), 1102 (f844), 1104 (a574), 1119 (172e),\n1120 (cb6f), 1121 (cc33), 1122 (ADR 0056), 1123 (e6bd), 1128 (172e tests), 1132 (8229), 1140 (a49e).\nNOTE 1121/1122 show an aggregate Verified -1 but ARE submittable: the operator's +1 satisfies the\nrequirement because Verified resolves on MAX VALUE, not max-with-block. Confirm with\n`/changes/<n>?o=SUBMIT_REQUIREMENTS`, which is the AUTHORITY — the aggregate label is misleading.\nTheir red is fully diagnosed and is NOT a defect: both fail exactly one test,\ntest_runner_regains_real_headroom_under_the_cap, because cc33 puts runner.py at 782 while the\nextraction paying it back sits above it in the stack. cc33 can never be green on its own patchset.\nDO NOT re-investigate or \"fix\" this.\n\nCLOSED (22): 7468 f184 2932 dc0d 9249 f844 cb6f cc33 1484 a574 281f 3a98 2682 1903 7f86 e6bd 8229\na49e, plus earlier epic bugs.\n\n================================================================================\nWHAT IS LEFT, IN ORDER\n================================================================================\n1. 7761 — Pass-1 ladder expressed in classes. THE NEXT TICKET. Plan came back BLOCK x7 and has just\n   been remediated (see \"7761 state\"). Confirm its review passes, claim it, implement.\n2. 172e — verifier paths resolve the standard class. PARTLY DONE: the plan-review verifier path is\n   done and green (change 1119) and the through-callers tests exist (1128). What REMAINS is the\n   CODE-REVIEW verifier path, a REAL functional gap verified in source:\n     - config.py:362 `resolve_model` is `return step or workflow or cfg.model`, no class resolution;\n     - workflow/gates/code-review.yaml:143-155 (the verify step) has NO `model:` key at all.\n   So code-review's Pass-2 verifier resolves cfg.model — the FRONTIER model — and adding\n   `model: standard` to that YAML today would pass the LITERAL STRING \"standard\" to the runner.\n   172e and 7761 SHARE call site (a) below: whichever lands first wires it. A blocking link\n   7761->172e exists in the store but its stated reason was WRONG (see corrections); treat it as a\n   shared prerequisite and re-examine the direction.\n3. d23e — deprecate REBAR_LLM_MODEL, ADR + example configs. Blocked by 172e and 7761.\n4. 343b gap 5 — provider_provenance never reaches a production payload: finalize_verdict\n   (plan_review/orchestrator.py:528) does not receive it. Change 1086 is MERGED but the ticket is\n   still in_progress precisely because of this.\n5. eb58 — the model-classes parent. Closes when f844/cb6f/172e/7761/cc33/d23e are done; that\n   unblocks S6 0d76 (Bedrock parity bar), S8 eb6e (the production cutover — OPERATOR-APPROVED,\n   lands after cc33), S9 8625 (ADR + docs).\n6. 5e75 — tracked follow-up: ADR 0056's uncovered build_agent_kwargs + the ~19-line telemetry\n   block. These are the two extractions that actually shrink run()'s METHOD body.\n7. THE EPIC CLOSES LAST, then the stack lands.\n\n================================================================================\n7761 STATE — read before touching it\n================================================================================\nIts MECHANISM section was FACTUALLY WRONG and has just been rewritten. It claimed resolution happens\nat \"the ONE funnel, config.resolve_model\". Parent eb58 comment 4 repudiates that (\"My 'ONE funnel'\nkeystone claim was WRONG\") and model_classes.py:238 documents \"THERE IS NO SINGLE INTERCEPTION\nPOINT\". The corrected model is ONE FUNCTION — model_classes.resolve_model_string, which f844 ALREADY\nDELIVERED AND TESTED — wired at several call sites:\n  a. config.resolve_model (config.py:362), sole caller workflow/runs.py:154. THE IMPORT MUST BE\n     LAZY, INSIDE THE FUNCTION BODY: model_classes imports config at module scope and its docstring\n     forbids the reverse, so a module-level import closes a cycle.\n  b. production_batch_runner.py:93 and code_review/batch_runner.py:55 (model_ladder[0] -> cfg.model)\n  c. workflow/runners.py:120, DefaultBatchRunner.run() — a THIRD ladder consumer with the identical\n     split-brain, found by this ticket's review and previously in NOBODY's scope.\n  d. the verifier paths (resolve_verifier_model, _verifier_cfg + xcheck.py:198/336) — 172e's.\n\nDESIGN DECISION now recorded in the plan: sizing.MODEL_LADDER stays EXACTLY as it is. It is a\n(BARE_NAME, WINDOW_TOKENS) table serving TWO jobs — escalation order via models_at_or_above\n(sizing.py:312) AND P8's context budget via largest_window_tokens (sizing.py:137-146), which matches\nwith `if name in model`, a SUBSTRING test. Putting class names in the table makes\n`\"standard\" in \"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6\"` FALSE, so largest_window_tokens silently\nreturns the ladder maximum for every model and P8's budget changes with no error. Instead: keep the\ntable, keep largest_window_tokens byte-for-byte, and have models_at_or_above find the start rung\nwith the existing substring match then map each at-or-above rung through the class config\n(haiku->trivial, sonnet->standard, opus->frontier). With no classes configured resolve_model_string\nreturns names unchanged, so today's bare-id behaviour is preserved.\n\n================================================================================\nCORRECTIONS TO EARLIER CLAIMS — do not re-inherit these mistakes\n================================================================================\n- \"resolve_model is the ONE funnel\" — WRONG, repudiated by eb58 comment 4 and model_classes.py:238.\n  It is the root of 6 of the 7 findings on 7761.\n- \"reviews must run against a clean worktree\" — WRONG and retracted. A dirty worktree does not\n  affect signing in attested mode; the real cause of false staleness was always the gate ref.\n- \"the CI bot's -1 vetoes the operator's +1\" — WRONG. Verified resolves on max value; query\n  SUBMIT_REQUIREMENTS, not the aggregate label.\n- 7f86's run() bound: set to 260, then 310, and it MEASURED 323. Twice optimistic. The criterion now\n  RECORDS the measurement rather than gating on it, with 5e75 owning the remainder.\n\n================================================================================\nOPERATIONAL TRAPS THAT COST REAL TIME\n================================================================================\n- FOUR staleness modes, different triggers: stale-code (a scoped dep differs from the GATE REF),\n  stale-material (this ticket's own description/AC/file_impact/children changed — `set-file-impact`\n  COUNTS and will destroy a good attestation), stale-pin-drift (a CHILD's plan edited after the\n  parent's review pinned it), stale-head (an UNSCOPED attestation, i.e. no file_impact, dies on\n  EVERY commit). COMMENTS ARE NOT MATERIAL and are always safe.\n- SET file_impact BEFORE reviewing, never after: it is a material edit and destroys a fresh PASS.\n- The COMPLETION VERIFIER CANNOT READ GIT HISTORY — repository file tools only. Any AC whose\n  evidence lives outside the codebase (a commit message, a Gerrit vote, a deploy) must be tagged\n  [operator-attested] or it burns a close-gate cycle. docs/plan-review-gate.md documents this and\n  has an advisory lint for it; the lint MISSES the case where you name a `git log ... | grep`\n  proving command, because that trips its codebase-verifiable suppression co-signal.\n- FIXING AN AC AT CLOSE TIME COSTS A FULL PLAN-REVIEW ROUND: the edit makes the plan attestation\n  stale-material and the close gate then demands a fresh review. Get ACs right at plan time.\n- CLOSING A COMPLETED STORY invites \"the plan describes work already done\" findings, because plans\n  are prospective. Convert the plan to DELIVERED state — a status banner with each child's commit\n  and change, the measured outcome, and an instruction to read the prospective sections as the\n  RECORD. That is what finally certified 7f86 after four rounds.\n- BUG closes require --class <regression|plan_defect|env_integration|flaky|preexisting|not_a_bug|\n  duplicate|escalated|undetermined>.\n- The commit-msg hook enforces 50/72: subject < 50 CHARS, body <= 72 BYTES per line. Em-dashes are\n  multibyte and will fail a line that looks short.\n- NEVER pipe a command whose exit code matters into tail/head — the pipeline's status is the last\n  stage's. This masked a real gate failure in this session.\n- Backticks inside a DOUBLE-QUOTED inline shell argument are command-substituted and will mangle or\n  strip ticket text. Write the body to a file and pass \"$(cat file)\" — substitution results are not\n  re-scanned, so that form is safe. eb58 comment 3 is a real casualty; comment 4 is its replacement.\n- STASHES: reference by MESSAGE, never by index — indices shift as other sessions stash. None of the\n  current stashes belong to this epic; leave them alone.\n- Always `rebar resolve <short-id>` before using a full ticket id in a command; do not reconstruct\n  one from memory.\n\n================================================================================\nLANDING THE REST\n================================================================================\nA plain `git rebase origin/main` now FAILS EARLY with add/add conflicts on our OWN landed work:\nGerrit's server-side rebase gave the merged commits new SHAs, so patch-id matching fails and git\nreplays our originals on top of themselves. DO NOT resolve those.\n\nCORRECT COMMAND:  git rebase --onto origin/main 96d683a0d\n(96d683a0d = 1903 = the last landed commit.) That replays only the un-landed commits and leaves\nEXACTLY ONE real conflict: gate_dispatch.py in commit 1484, 3 hunks. A genuine semantic clash —\n1484 extracts 320 lines into plan_review_recovery.py while main's 2c89 independently cut 252 from\nthe same file — so 2c89's \"cancel a mid-run review on own-plan edits\" behaviour must be carried INTO\nthe extracted module, not dropped.\n\nDO THIS ONCE, IMMEDIATELY BEFORE LANDING. Rebasing forces new patchsets on every remaining change\nand drops every vote, including the operator's manual Verified overrides on 1121/1122.\n\nPer-firing loop: (1) check Gerrit votes, re-push/rebase anything conflicted, but IGNORE a\nVerified -1 that is only the module-size gate — the operator overrides those ancestors manually;\nkeep the TOP of the stack clean. (2) Advance the next unblocked ticket in dependency order.\n(3) Close any ticket whose change is Verified +1 and whose criteria are met. (4) Land only once the\nepic closes and every change is green — EXCEPT under the standing no-regression authorization,\nwhich permits landing a green prefix after an explicit regression check.\n\nHOW TO RUN THAT REGRESSION CHECK (this is what licensed the 10-change land): rebase the candidate\nprefix onto current main in a scratch worktree, run the full suite there, and RUN A CONTROL of any\nfailures against PLAIN origin/main before calling anything a regression — 12 errors looked like a\nregression and were a probe artifact. Then diff the LLM capability decisions for this project's\nactual model strings between main and main+prefix; wire-identical is the bar.\n\n================================================================================\nHOW TO WORK A TICKET HERE\n================================================================================\nCLAIM BEFORE YOU WORK — the claim gate is what forces a passing plan review, and building ahead of\nit risks throwing the work away when review moves the plan. Then held-out TDD: author the full test\nset yourself, confirm RED for the right reason, hand the implementation subagent ONLY the happy-path\ntests (physically remove the rest from its tree), then validate against the held-out ones and\nmutation-check that they actually bite. Every code leaf in this epic was done that way and the\nmutations caught real defects each time.\n\nDo NOT relax the <700 runner.py headroom guard, and do NOT delete comment content to fit under it.\nCompress wording only; never drop a measured number, a file:line citation, a bug/story id, a named\nfailure mode, or a stated invariant. Deleting documentation to satisfy a size guard is the same\nspecies of move as raising the cap, which ADR 0056 rejects by name.\n\nA KNOWN LIVE GAP, INDEPENDENT OF THIS EPIC, worth its own ticket: code-review's Pass-2\n`temperature: 0` has NEVER taken effect. Code review has no verifier downgrade so all its passes run\non opus, whose temperature pydantic-ai's Anthropic adapter silently drops (models/anthropic.py:641).\nThe gate reviewing this epic has never had the greedy determinism its config claims."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785543445935375001,"uuid":"fbfffbe7-b4bd-47f9-aa8a-37aeda7fda5c"}